      "content": "While $p=0.042$ suggests significance, it only suggests the probability of observing the data given the null hypothesis is 4.2%, not the probability the null hypothesis is true. To discuss noise and unseen factors, researchers typically acknowledge limitations such as potential sample bias or measurement error when discussing practical significance. You can discuss the bounds of uncertainty using the confidence interval, noting that the true effect is *likely* within [0.12, 0.85], representing the remaining sampling uncertainty.",
